---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/alternatives/activecampaign-alternative';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives/activecampaign-alternative');
const desc = description(site, 'alternatives/activecampaign-alternative');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When is staying with ActiveCampaign useful?",
    "answer": "Stay when account relationships, sales follow-up and marketing automation share useful context. Repair a specific cart trigger, exclusion or ownership rule before replacing a working CRM."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a cart sender replace CRM?",
    "answer": "Only if the receiving team can demonstrate the account, contact, deal and sales task workflow you actually use. Sending a recovery email does not transfer quote approval or rep ownership."
  },
  {
    "question": "What determines cutover readiness?",
    "answer": "Reconcile account and contact identifiers, permissions, open sales work, current purchase state and queued journeys. Assign one sender and retain a rollback ledger; elapsed days alone do not establish readiness."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should discounts be compared?",
    "answer": "For an illustrative $61 order at 46% gross margin, goods cost stays $32.94. A 13% selling-price discount reduces contribution from $28.06 to $20.13 before other variable costs. Measure incremental contribution separately from attributed revenue."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-16 md:py-24 container">
    <a href="/alternatives" class="link text-sm font-bold">← All alternatives</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">ActiveCampaign alternatives: preserve the sales handover</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-xl text-slate-700">Decide whether to repair Shopify recovery inside ActiveCampaign, retain CRM while changing the sender, or replace both with an explicitly owned sales workflow.</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-10">
      <h2>Start with the Shopify and CRM boundary</h2>
      <p>ActiveCampaign documents <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360012461919-Get-started-with-the-ActiveCampaign-Shopify-integration">Shopify purchase and abandoned-cart data for guests and logged-in customers</a>. That is a useful starting point for a store combining ecommerce with sales follow-up. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/14251311251356-Overview-of-ActiveCampaign-plans">plan overview</a> separates base plans from enhanced CRM and other add-ons. Compare the account's actual entitlements, seats, contact basis and connected services, rather than assuming every CRM feature is included or inventing a universal replacement price.</p>
      <p>If carts reach the right contact but the wrong branch sends, repair enrollment, purchase exits or rep exclusions in the existing automation. If the CRM is valuable but recovery is operationally awkward, keep account and sales records there while evaluating another sender. Replace both only after the receiving operator accepts the open sales work. These are alternative project scopes, not a ranking.</p>
      <h2>Separate account, contact and sales authority</h2>
      <p>Make a proposed handover sheet with the Shopify customer ID, contact ID, account ID where used, deal ID, owner, quote version, task due state and the person allowed to approve an offer. An account can have several contacts: an employee abandoning a cart is not necessarily the buyer authorized to accept a negotiated quote. A rep's task completion is not a purchase, and an account relationship is not permission to email every contact.</p>
      <p>Keep channel and purpose permissions with their source, timestamp and withdrawals. Decide which system can update each field and how conflicts are resolved. A later import must not reopen a withdrawn permission. Preserve reply routing to the responsible rep, and reconcile reassignment or a departed employee before enabling automated follow-up. Retain CRM if recreating those relationships would remove useful sales context.</p>
      <h2>Accept the queued journey before moving it</h2>
      <p>Inventory every active automation and external caller that can create a cart message, deal task or rep notification. For each queued contact, record its current branch, last actual send, next eligible action and whether a human quote is pending. The receiving operator should accept that state explicitly. A bulk contact import must not restart every journey from the beginning.</p>
      <p>Pause new enrollment in the outgoing recovery path, reconcile current purchases and withdrawals, then choose to drain or cancel its queued messages. Give each cart and channel one sending owner. Keep stable cart, customer and event identifiers so a delayed purchase or replay cannot create a second journey. Resolve welcome and SMS collisions against the shared send ledger, not separate campaign totals.</p>
      <h2>Proposed merchant acceptance cases</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>For a mobile interruption, restore the current cart on another device without losing the rep's quote reference. A completed purchase must exit recovery.</li>
        <li>For shipping shock or a financing interruption, route the customer to the relevant shipping explanation or payment help; do not substitute a discount for unresolved terms.</li>
        <li>For gift sizing, preserve the selected variant and a route to advice. If the variant is unavailable, suppress stale product claims and have the owner approve a substitute.</li>
        <li>For a minimum-order block, distinguish retail checkout from a negotiated order. A cart email must not override the rep's minimum quantity or quote authority.</li>
        <li>For a stacked coupon or repeat purchaser, check eligibility and existing incentives before presenting another offer. Record the approved final terms in both the sales handover and sending decision.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Run these as proposed fixtures with the intended sender and receiving rep; they are not tests performed here. Include an opt-out after enrollment, a purchase arriving late, and a duplicate event. Acceptance means the responsible operator can explain the resulting send, suppression and sales task, including an uncertain case held for review.</p>
      <h2>Economics and alternatives by job</h2>
      <p>Illustrative fixed goods cost: a $61 order at 46% gross margin has $32.94 goods cost and $28.06 full-price contribution. A 13% selling-price discount leaves $53.07 revenue and $20.13 contribution, a $7.93 reduction before payment fees, shipping support, returns and software or rep labor. Keep the same goods cost in both paths. A recovered order attributed to a message does not establish incremental recovery; use a suitably sized comparison cohort where feasible and report uncertainty.</p>
      <p>For a Shopify sender evaluation, use <a class="link" href="/alternatives/klaviyo-alternative">Klaviyo</a> or <a class="link" href="/alternatives/omnisend-alternative">Omnisend</a> as research paths against your event and queue requirements. For a separate SMS scope, evaluate <a class="link" href="/alternatives/postscript-alternative">Postscript</a> or <a class="link" href="/alternatives/recart-alternative">Recart</a> with the same purchase and permission fixtures. <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-klaviyo">Sequenzy</a> is another candidate if its demonstrated workflow fits the lifecycle work you want to simplify; it is not a presumed replacement for account hierarchy, quotes or rep tasks.</p>
      <h2>Rollback follows actual sends</h2>
      <p>Keep a reversible mapping and an export of the accepted open-work inventory. If identity, quote authority or purchase exits fail, pause the new path and reconcile actual sends before restoring the old one. Do not resume an old queue blindly: remove contacts already messaged, purchased or withdrawn, preserve completed rep work, and record who owns unresolved exceptions. Choose rollout pace from verified behavior and enough measurement data, rather than an arbitrary calendar promise.</p>
    </div>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="FAQ: Activecampaign alternatives" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
